{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>On the day his Nazirite vow is completed.</b> The reason for this offering of the Nazirite on the day his Nazirite vow is completed has not been revealed, for we do not find in all the Torah any offering except for a sin that has already passed; but this one preceded and merited to perform a commandment, and all the days of his Naziriteship he was holy and separated from the desires of the world, and now on the day his vow is completed the Torah obligates him to bring an offering — this is a wondrous and novel matter. And so the Sages, of blessed memory, said in tractate Nazir: it is a novelty (חידוש) that the Torah innovated regarding the Nazirite." "<b>And the Ramban,</b> of blessed memory, gave a reason for it by way of the plain sense (peshat) and said: this man sins against his soul upon completing the Naziriteship, for he now departs from the holiness and service of the blessed Name, and it would have been fitting for him to remain a Nazirite forever and to stand all his days in his holiness, a Nazirite and holy to his Elohim, in the manner of what is said (Amos 2:11): ״ואקים מבניכם לנביאים ומבחוריכם לנזירים״ — the verse equates him with the prophet; and as it is written: ״כל ימי נזרו קדוש הוא לאלהיו״; and therefore he requires atonement upon returning to be defiled by the desires of the world. Thus far the language of the Rabbi, of blessed memory. And despite all this reasoning, the offering of the Nazirite does not escape from being a novelty, for where do we find an offering for the future?" "<b>Rather,</b> the reason for the offering and the novelty is clarified only by way of the Kabbalah. And I have already mentioned in the passage on his Naziriteship that he is above the attributes (מדות) and cleaves to the essence of the supernal mercies; and now he wishes to depart from his holiness — he appears as one moving away and wishing, heaven forbid, to remove himself from that degree to which he had already cleaved. Therefore the Torah required of him an offering on the day of completion, and not for atonement, for no expression of atonement or pardon is found in it; but the intention of his offering is to draw near the forces (כוחות), to unify them, and to draw down for them overflow (שפע) from the Source, so that the attributes (מדות) are supplied, and afterward he may return to his enjoyments as at first. And therefore the offering he was commanded comprised an elevation-offering (עולה) and peace-offerings (שלמים) and a sin-offering (חטאת): a lamb for the elevation-offering, a ram for the peace-offerings, a ewe-lamb for the sin-offering. And at the end it says: ״ואחר ישתה הנזיר יין״ — that is, after he completes his obligation on every side and the attributes are supplied, the Nazirite may drink wine and return to his enjoyments according to the way of the children of men in the world of bodies."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:30:03.768147131Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}